  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nA shortwave trough continues to push into the Northern Plains this \nafternoon. No rain is associated with the wave at this time but it \nis bringing thicker, high level cirrus with it. This cirrus has kept \ntemperatures on track with highs maxing out in the 50s and 60s.\nAs the wave pushes through the forecast area tonight, thermal \nprofiles will cool to saturation in the dendritic growth zone \n(DGZ). However, thermal profiles below the DGZ look to be dry. \nThus, little if any rain will reach the surface. If it does, it \nwill be no more than some sprinkles. A cold front will push \nthrough the area tonight due to it being tied to the wave. The \ncloud cover and elevated winds behind the front will keep low \ntemperatures on the warm side with lows only falling to the \nupper 30s and low 40s. \n\nTemperatures will remain cooler than what we have seen from the \nweekend and Monday with highs falling to the upper 40s and 50s on \nWednesday. A second cold front and associated push of cold air \nadvection (CAA) will push through the area on Wednesday, resulting \nin temperatures falling to near normal for the rest of the work \nweek. Lows will bottom out in the 20s to low 30s with highs only \nreaching into the 40s. Winds will be light on Thursday as surface \nhigh pressure pushes through the area. But breezy winds look to \nreturn to the area along and west of the James River on Friday. This \narea looks to see wind chills below 40F for the day.\n\nTemperatures look to moderate to near to slightly above normal for \nthe weekend as upper level ridging pushes eastwards. Highs will \nremain in the 40s to low 50s with lows falling to the 30s. Saturday \nwill be dry but a shortwave trough will push through the central and \nsouthern Plains on Sunday. Some wrap around moisture is possible \nfrom this system as is somewhat supported by the ensembles. They \nshow a close to zero probability for rain totals to exceed a tenth \nof an inch. The GFS ensemble is the outlier as it shows the highest \nprobabilities up to about a 40% chance for exceeding the same amount \nof QPF. If any rain does make it to the forecast area, it looks to \nbe very light at this time.\n\nTemperature trends look to persist into early next week. Medium \nrange guidance continues to vary regarding how the upper level \npattern will evolve through early next week. Broad troughing along \nthe western CONUS could push eastwards and bring chances for rain \nback to the area. Too uncertain to make any changes to the forecast \nbut something to keep an eye on.",
